WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.923
- It's a magic book art.

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:06.340
I am one of those rather strange artists,

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:09.600
who paint pictures that people can't see;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.580
it's a discreet painting,

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:14.787
it's only there when you
know how to unlock it,

00:00:14.787 --> 00:00:17.383
now you see it, now you don't.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.990
My name is Martin Frost,

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:30.760
I am what's known as a fore-edge painter,

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:34.040
so I paint pictures on the edges of books,

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:37.500
but I do it in such a way,
that they become invisible,

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.460
unless you fan the books
up in a particular way

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:42.689
and then you un-fan the
book, and it vanishes again;

00:00:42.689 --> 00:00:45.920
they're gone, vanished,

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:48.543
because the gold has hidden the image.

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:52.840
It is pretty much a British,
if not English art form.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:57.060
Vanishing fore-edge paintings
date back to about 1660.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.933
It really got going
back in the 18th century

00:00:59.933 --> 00:01:01.823
when it became quite popular.

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:04.900
I was working in illustration,

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:08.430
and I met a fellow, who
was a fore-edge painter,

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:11.050
and he showed me what he
was doing and I liked that,

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:13.787
I thought, "that looks
fun, I could do that."

00:01:13.787 --> 00:01:17.420
And that, as I said was
over 40 years ago now.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:21.305
Most of the work I've done
has been on antique books.

00:01:21.305 --> 00:01:22.740
I'll show you this one:

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.510
Now here, we have here
a large Shakespeare;

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:28.980
it's 1860 leather-bound, split double,

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.633
two different images on one book.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.396
This is a book entitled "Desert Isles."

00:01:35.396 --> 00:01:37.754
It was produced in the 1920s.

00:01:37.754 --> 00:01:40.430
This is known as a two-way double,

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:43.100
one one way and one the other,

00:01:44.388 --> 00:01:49.110
and both of them will be
hidden under the gold.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:52.601
I'm often asked by people,
"And how long does this take?"

00:01:52.601 --> 00:01:54.530
"How long is that piece of string?"

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:56.510
If you're working on a very small book,

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:58.840
it will take you a few hours,

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:00.980
generally speaking, for a typical book

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:04.504
of about that sort of size,
a day-and-a-half, two days;

00:02:04.504 --> 00:02:07.220
if you're working on
something like a big Bible,

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:08.913
it's not difficult to use a week.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:13.316
The price reflects the
time that goes into it;

00:02:13.316 --> 00:02:15.640
the bigger Bibles, the big ones,

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:19.183
ooh, six, £700, something like that.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.896
Ah, yes,

00:02:24.896 --> 00:02:27.793
another Harry Potter,
let me just open it up.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:33.273
Gone.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.410
I find these quite satisfying.

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:38.660
As far as I know, I am the only

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:41.700
commercial fore-edge painter working,

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:44.050
and so long as my eyes hang on in there

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:47.070
and my hands, I'll continue,

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:50.950
but if I'm ever asked to describe
myself, I say "I'm a dad,"

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:53.050
I'm a grandad and I'm a fore-edge painter,

00:02:54.644 --> 00:02:56.144
that works for me.

